1
00:00:00,466 --> 00:00:02,866
You just filled tomorrow with twenty time slots

2
00:00:02,933 --> 00:00:04,433
Your anxiety instantly felt half as heavy

3
00:00:04,766 --> 00:00:08,500
But what is really holding you back may be this hardworking looking schedule

4
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:09,800
Read at seven in the morning

5
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:11,066
Organize materials at eight

6
00:00:11,133 --> 00:00:12,400
Start work at nine

7
00:00:12,466 --> 00:00:13,866
Exercise in the evening

8
00:00:13,866 --> 00:00:14,500
Review

9
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Study

10
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:15,733
Edit videos

11
00:00:15,766 --> 00:00:17,200
Looking at the packed schedule

12
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:19,066
you feel life is finally back in your hands

13
00:00:19,066 --> 00:00:20,133
But at nine the next day

14
00:00:20,133 --> 00:00:21,233
you sit at your desk

15
00:00:21,266 --> 00:00:22,300
Pour yourself a glass of water first

16
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:23,400
then tweak the font

17
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:24,866
then change the background music

18
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:26,000
Twenty minutes pass

19
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:27,000
The schedule is full

20
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:28,166
The document is still blank

21
00:00:28,166 --> 00:00:29,700
This does not necessarily mean you lack discipline

22
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:33,300
More likely you got the sense of control that should come only after taking action

23
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:35,400
The brain easily mistakes planning for progress

24
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:37,800
and feeling less anxious for things getting better

25
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:39,666
So the more complete your plan becomes

26
00:00:39,666 --> 00:00:41,966
the bigger the gap when you finally start

27
00:00:41,966 --> 00:00:44,133
This video is not telling you to stop planning

28
00:00:44,133 --> 00:00:46,133
It only wants to help you see one boundary

29
00:00:46,133 --> 00:00:48,000
A plan that gets you moving faster

30
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:49,900
is action avoidance that keeps you preparing

31
00:00:49,900 --> 00:00:52,000
After watching you only need one extra question

32
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Can this time slot leave a real trace within ten minutes

33
00:00:56,133 --> 00:00:57,833
You have probably seen this scene

34
00:00:58,300 --> 00:01:00,000
Someone buys a new notebook

35
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,233
The cover is spotless

36
00:01:01,266 --> 00:01:02,433
The paper feels smooth

37
00:01:02,700 --> 00:01:04,400
The first page says goals for this year

38
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:06,033
The second page says monthly plan

39
00:01:06,166 --> 00:01:08,000
The third page says tasks for this week

40
00:01:08,166 --> 00:01:09,433
The handwriting is careful

41
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:10,933
The colors are carefully assigned

42
00:01:11,166 --> 00:01:13,966
It looks like life is finally about to start over

43
00:01:14,166 --> 00:01:15,266
But three days later

44
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:17,666
the notebook is pushed into the corner of the desk

45
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:19,400
with a delivery label on top

46
00:01:19,666 --> 00:01:20,900
Another week passes

47
00:01:20,900 --> 00:01:22,133
and it becomes a coaster

48
00:01:22,466 --> 00:01:24,633
This is not because the person is beyond saving

49
00:01:24,766 --> 00:01:27,600
It is because plans naturally offer powerful comfort

50
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:29,600
When you turn chaos into a list

51
00:01:29,966 --> 00:01:31,266
schedule your anxiety

52
00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:33,066
and turn a pile of unclear things

53
00:01:33,066 --> 00:01:34,633
into a series of little boxes

54
00:01:34,766 --> 00:01:35,933
you briefly feel

55
00:01:35,933 --> 00:01:37,833
that life is back in your hands

56
00:01:38,033 --> 00:01:38,733
The problem is

57
00:01:38,733 --> 00:01:40,366
this feeling is too comfortable

58
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:41,933
comfortable enough to make you think

59
00:01:42,066 --> 00:01:43,800
you have already started getting better

60
00:01:44,066 --> 00:01:45,933
You only wrote down go running

61
00:01:46,066 --> 00:01:47,766
but your body is still in the chair

62
00:01:47,966 --> 00:01:49,866
You only saved a course

63
00:01:50,066 --> 00:01:52,000
but your mind has not truly processed it

64
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:54,600
You only renamed a folder

65
00:01:54,700 --> 00:01:56,233
but the work still does not exist

66
00:01:56,533 --> 00:01:59,133
This is where procrastination hides most carefully

67
00:01:59,300 --> 00:02:01,366
It is not lying in bed doing nothing

68
00:02:01,533 --> 00:02:03,600
It often wears the clothes of serious work

69
00:02:03,766 --> 00:02:04,833
You organize materials

70
00:02:04,866 --> 00:02:06,033
You optimize your tools

71
00:02:06,066 --> 00:02:07,200
You study methods

72
00:02:07,266 --> 00:02:08,933
You keep adjusting your schedule

73
00:02:09,100 --> 00:02:11,133
From the outside it looks like you are working hard

74
00:02:11,266 --> 00:02:12,600
You also tell yourself

75
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:13,600
I am not doing nothing

76
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:14,800
I am just preparing

77
00:02:15,100 --> 00:02:16,366
But preparation

78
00:02:16,466 --> 00:02:18,533
once it does not lead to a concrete action

79
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:20,633
turns into sophisticated procrastination

80
00:02:21,066 --> 00:02:23,400
If a plan gets you to act right away

81
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:24,533
it is clearly useful

82
00:02:24,733 --> 00:02:26,866
This is not a video against planning either

83
00:02:27,300 --> 00:02:29,466
What you really need to watch for is another moment

84
00:02:29,966 --> 00:02:31,133
The more detailed the plan becomes

85
00:02:31,300 --> 00:02:33,233
the task does not move forward even a millimeter

86
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:35,633
At that point you may be scheduling

87
00:02:35,733 --> 00:02:38,466
a safe zone where you do not have to face the result yet

88
00:02:38,733 --> 00:02:40,333
You are not moving closer to the result

89
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:43,200
You are moving closer to the feeling that you are in control

90
00:02:43,533 --> 00:02:45,266
The hardest thing for many people to admit is

91
00:02:45,333 --> 00:02:47,066
they do not actually hate hard work

92
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:49,333
They are afraid that once they truly begin

93
00:02:49,366 --> 00:02:51,133
they will immediately see the limits of their ability

94
00:02:51,366 --> 00:02:53,000
A schedule will not reject you

95
00:02:53,066 --> 00:02:54,866
A to do app will not judge you

96
00:02:54,900 --> 00:02:56,766
A new course will not embarrass you

97
00:02:56,966 --> 00:02:58,766
But writing the first paragraph

98
00:02:58,866 --> 00:03:00,433
recording the first take

99
00:03:00,533 --> 00:03:02,133
building the first draft of a proposal

100
00:03:02,200 --> 00:03:03,333
will immediately tell you

101
00:03:03,333 --> 00:03:04,233
what you do not know

102
00:03:04,266 --> 00:03:05,233
where you are stuck

103
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:06,933
what you never really thought through

104
00:03:07,100 --> 00:03:09,400
So people instinctively retreat into planning

105
00:03:09,466 --> 00:03:11,133
because there is no failure inside a plan

106
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:13,433
Inside a plan there is only a version of yourself that has not started yet

107
00:03:13,733 --> 00:03:15,700
That version looks full of promise

108
00:03:15,700 --> 00:03:16,633
and safe

109
00:03:17,100 --> 00:03:18,566
Psychology has a term for this

110
00:03:18,966 --> 00:03:20,333
the illusion of control

111
00:03:20,766 --> 00:03:21,933
In plain language

112
00:03:22,200 --> 00:03:23,933
when people face uncertainty

113
00:03:24,200 --> 00:03:26,600
they easily mistake small actions they can control

114
00:03:26,733 --> 00:03:28,866
for actually controlling the outcome

115
00:03:29,366 --> 00:03:31,333
You cannot guarantee you will feel good tomorrow

116
00:03:31,566 --> 00:03:33,166
but you can fill tomorrow with tasks

117
00:03:33,533 --> 00:03:35,533
You cannot guarantee you will finish the article

118
00:03:35,733 --> 00:03:37,566
but you can find ten references first

119
00:03:37,933 --> 00:03:39,966
You cannot guarantee anyone will watch the video

120
00:03:40,166 --> 00:03:41,633
but you can adjust the thumbnail first

121
00:03:42,166 --> 00:03:43,933
None of these actions is wrong by itself

122
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:45,400
The problem is

123
00:03:45,500 --> 00:03:48,000
they are so good at giving you a false sense of progress

124
00:03:48,300 --> 00:03:50,166
It is like standing at the gym entrance

125
00:03:50,333 --> 00:03:51,433
already changed into workout clothes

126
00:03:51,566 --> 00:03:52,666
holding your water bottle

127
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:54,166
and even taking a photo

128
00:03:54,566 --> 00:03:56,466
This whole routine looks a lot like exercising

129
00:03:56,700 --> 00:03:59,600
but muscles do not grow just because you dress like a professional

130
00:03:59,966 --> 00:04:01,166
Learning works the same way

131
00:04:01,333 --> 00:04:02,433
You open the course

132
00:04:02,600 --> 00:04:03,566
make an outline

133
00:04:03,700 --> 00:04:04,666
mark the key points

134
00:04:04,866 --> 00:04:07,433
and add every book the teacher mentioned to your cart

135
00:04:07,733 --> 00:04:09,433
This is certainly better than ignoring everything

136
00:04:09,766 --> 00:04:11,600
But if you never try to explain it from memory

137
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:13,733
never apply one idea to a real problem

138
00:04:13,933 --> 00:04:16,366
and never explain one point to someone else

139
00:04:16,666 --> 00:04:18,833
you have only put knowledge on a shelf

140
00:04:19,066 --> 00:04:20,833
It has not entered your judgment yet

141
00:04:20,933 --> 00:04:22,600
The most dangerous thing about planning

142
00:04:22,600 --> 00:04:23,666
is not that it is useless

143
00:04:23,800 --> 00:04:25,333
but that it looks so useful

144
00:04:25,566 --> 00:04:26,733
It gives you order

145
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:27,933
gives you a sense of safety

146
00:04:28,066 --> 00:04:30,366
gives you a clear posture for starting

147
00:04:30,666 --> 00:04:31,966
But real action

148
00:04:31,966 --> 00:04:33,533
often looks ugly at the beginning

149
00:04:33,800 --> 00:04:35,233
The first draft will be messy

150
00:04:35,400 --> 00:04:36,933
The first take will be full of stumbles

151
00:04:37,100 --> 00:04:38,733
The first proposal will be naive

152
00:04:38,866 --> 00:04:40,200
The first workout will leave you breathless

153
00:04:40,466 --> 00:04:41,666
These ugly things

154
00:04:41,866 --> 00:04:43,233
are real progress

155
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:45,633
because they expose the problem

156
00:04:45,666 --> 00:04:47,766
As long as someone stays inside the plan

157
00:04:47,866 --> 00:04:50,066
they are facing an imagined task

158
00:04:50,333 --> 00:04:52,133
Imagined tasks are obedient

159
00:04:52,333 --> 00:04:53,866
They follow your schedule

160
00:04:54,066 --> 00:04:55,400
They do not suddenly become harder

161
00:04:55,566 --> 00:04:57,366
or expose what you do not know

162
00:04:57,600 --> 00:04:59,133
But real tasks are different

163
00:04:59,333 --> 00:05:00,800
Real tasks talk back

164
00:05:00,966 --> 00:05:03,133
You discover you lack enough material while writing

165
00:05:03,300 --> 00:05:05,366
You find the logic is off while recording

166
00:05:05,566 --> 00:05:08,233
You realize you never understood the earlier concept while studying

167
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:10,966
You find you overestimated your time while working

168
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:13,433
That is when you truly enter the task

169
00:05:13,533 --> 00:05:14,633
And only then

170
00:05:14,766 --> 00:05:16,400
does the task begin to change you

171
00:05:16,566 --> 00:05:18,400
So it is not that you cannot plan

172
00:05:18,666 --> 00:05:20,033
The problem with a plan is

173
00:05:20,066 --> 00:05:21,733
it must serve action

174
00:05:21,766 --> 00:05:23,166
rather than replace action

175
00:05:23,366 --> 00:05:24,766
If a schedule

176
00:05:24,766 --> 00:05:26,066
helps you start faster

177
00:05:26,133 --> 00:05:27,166
then it is a tool

178
00:05:27,400 --> 00:05:28,766
If a schedule

179
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:31,366
keeps making you feel you should prepare a little more

180
00:05:31,500 --> 00:05:33,233
it has become an escape route

181
00:05:33,500 --> 00:05:35,000
Someone decides to learn English

182
00:05:35,166 --> 00:05:37,466
They first research the best vocabulary app

183
00:05:37,800 --> 00:05:39,133
watch ten reviews

184
00:05:39,133 --> 00:05:40,600
then look up the forgetting curve

185
00:05:40,900 --> 00:05:43,166
next they compare paper books and ebooks

186
00:05:43,466 --> 00:05:45,133
By the time they choose a tool

187
00:05:45,133 --> 00:05:46,533
a week has passed

188
00:05:46,700 --> 00:05:48,033
The words they actually memorized

189
00:05:48,166 --> 00:05:50,500
may be only the twenty they tried on day one

190
00:05:50,500 --> 00:05:51,933
Someone decides to edit videos

191
00:05:52,266 --> 00:05:54,066
They first save camera movement tutorials

192
00:05:54,300 --> 00:05:55,800
then study subtitle styles

193
00:05:55,966 --> 00:05:57,633
then watch how others make thumbnails

194
00:05:57,866 --> 00:05:59,966
They sort their media folders very carefully

195
00:06:00,100 --> 00:06:00,900
Interviews

196
00:06:00,966 --> 00:06:01,800
Cinematic

197
00:06:01,900 --> 00:06:02,933
City nights

198
00:06:02,966 --> 00:06:04,000
Portrait closeups

199
00:06:04,166 --> 00:06:05,966
Every folder is spotless

200
00:06:06,100 --> 00:06:07,866
but the timeline is still empty

201
00:06:07,866 --> 00:06:09,200
Someone decides to read

202
00:06:09,366 --> 00:06:10,633
They first look at book lists

203
00:06:10,866 --> 00:06:11,833
ten must reads

204
00:06:11,866 --> 00:06:12,966
twenty for the next level

205
00:06:13,066 --> 00:06:14,566
one hundred that will change your life

206
00:06:14,800 --> 00:06:16,066
The bookshelf gets fuller and fuller

207
00:06:16,100 --> 00:06:17,133
But the books they actually finish

208
00:06:17,133 --> 00:06:18,533
always stop at chapter one

209
00:06:18,800 --> 00:06:20,366
These scenes feel familiar

210
00:06:20,466 --> 00:06:22,266
because they all share one thing

211
00:06:22,533 --> 00:06:23,733
They keep you busy

212
00:06:23,866 --> 00:06:25,566
but do not necessarily move you forward

213
00:06:25,766 --> 00:06:26,566
Being busy and making progress

214
00:06:26,566 --> 00:06:27,600
are not the same thing

215
00:06:27,866 --> 00:06:29,400
Being busy means you spent time

216
00:06:29,600 --> 00:06:31,100
Progress means the task changed

217
00:06:31,100 --> 00:06:33,000
in some irreversible way because of you

218
00:06:33,200 --> 00:06:35,133
You write an opening you are not happy with

219
00:06:35,200 --> 00:06:36,000
It is ugly

220
00:06:36,100 --> 00:06:37,266
but it now exists

221
00:06:37,466 --> 00:06:39,233
You record a stumbling audio take

222
00:06:39,300 --> 00:06:40,100
It is not good

223
00:06:40,133 --> 00:06:41,833
but it shows you what sounds awkward

224
00:06:42,100 --> 00:06:44,000
You make a very ordinary proposal page

225
00:06:44,066 --> 00:06:45,033
It is not impressive

226
00:06:45,100 --> 00:06:47,133
but now someone can finally give you feedback

227
00:06:47,300 --> 00:06:48,866
Progress is often not pretty

228
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:50,433
while planning looks polished

229
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:52,833
That is why people prefer plans

230
00:06:53,100 --> 00:06:55,433
Plans let you keep looking like a smart person

231
00:06:55,666 --> 00:06:57,833
Action turns you back into a beginner

232
00:06:57,933 --> 00:06:59,733
The hardest thing for an adult

233
00:06:59,966 --> 00:07:01,066
is not lacking a method

234
00:07:01,200 --> 00:07:03,066
but admitting that when they start

235
00:07:03,200 --> 00:07:04,266
they will truly be clumsy

236
00:07:04,333 --> 00:07:06,000
You are writing a proposal at the office

237
00:07:06,100 --> 00:07:07,566
the cursor blinks for ten minutes

238
00:07:07,700 --> 00:07:08,633
You suddenly think

239
00:07:08,700 --> 00:07:10,466
Maybe I should look up more material

240
00:07:10,666 --> 00:07:11,800
You pick up your phone

241
00:07:11,866 --> 00:07:13,466
and that search takes half an hour

242
00:07:13,700 --> 00:07:15,533
On the surface you are improving the information

243
00:07:15,700 --> 00:07:17,533
In reality you are avoiding the first sentence

244
00:07:17,666 --> 00:07:18,766
You want to build an account

245
00:07:18,900 --> 00:07:20,566
You watch lots of breakdowns of viral videos

246
00:07:20,666 --> 00:07:22,766
The more you watch the less ready you feel

247
00:07:22,900 --> 00:07:24,433
On the surface you are learning

248
00:07:24,533 --> 00:07:26,400
In reality you are using other peoples maturity

249
00:07:26,466 --> 00:07:27,966
to scare your first version away

250
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:29,066
Real change

251
00:07:29,133 --> 00:07:31,433
usually does not begin with a perfect plan

252
00:07:31,566 --> 00:07:33,933
It begins with an action that does not look very good

253
00:07:34,066 --> 00:07:35,833
For example write just one hundred words today

254
00:07:35,900 --> 00:07:36,933
edit just ten seconds

255
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:38,066
memorize just five words

256
00:07:38,266 --> 00:07:40,566
explain one idea clearly to just one friend

257
00:07:40,700 --> 00:07:43,066
You can even write this action on paper

258
00:07:43,200 --> 00:07:45,066
Today is not about finishing the whole thing

259
00:07:45,200 --> 00:07:47,366
Today is only about giving it an opening

260
00:07:47,500 --> 00:07:48,933
One opening is enough

261
00:07:48,966 --> 00:07:51,466
Because what truly scares people about big tasks

262
00:07:51,500 --> 00:07:52,666
is not how difficult they are

263
00:07:52,800 --> 00:07:54,666
but that they stay inside the mind

264
00:07:54,800 --> 00:07:56,200
Once they land on paper

265
00:07:56,266 --> 00:07:57,533
land in the timeline

266
00:07:57,533 --> 00:07:59,333
or land in a real voice recording

267
00:07:59,333 --> 00:08:01,633
they are no longer just a thought pressing down on you

268
00:08:01,700 --> 00:08:03,733
These actions are so small they have no ceremony

269
00:08:03,800 --> 00:08:05,266
But they have one advantage

270
00:08:05,400 --> 00:08:07,533
They pull you out of imagination

271
00:08:07,533 --> 00:08:09,000
and into the real world

272
00:08:09,066 --> 00:08:10,566
The real world is not gentle

273
00:08:10,566 --> 00:08:11,866
but it gives feedback

274
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:12,866
And feedback

275
00:08:12,933 --> 00:08:15,033
is what makes action truly valuable

276
00:08:15,366 --> 00:08:16,566
It is ten thirty at night

277
00:08:16,733 --> 00:08:18,366
You open the to do app again

278
00:08:18,533 --> 00:08:20,700
ready to carry the ten unfinished tasks from today

279
00:08:20,700 --> 00:08:22,066
over to tomorrow unchanged

280
00:08:22,366 --> 00:08:23,666
Do not rush to copy them yet

281
00:08:23,766 --> 00:08:25,400
and do not rush to fill tomorrow again

282
00:08:25,566 --> 00:08:27,033
Do not blame yourself right away

283
00:08:27,200 --> 00:08:29,133
and do not pump yourself up right away either

284
00:08:29,266 --> 00:08:30,466
Ask yourself just one question

285
00:08:30,766 --> 00:08:33,433
What is the smallest ten minute action for this task

286
00:08:33,566 --> 00:08:35,233
It is not about changing your life today

287
00:08:35,333 --> 00:08:37,066
It is not about making a comeback this month

288
00:08:37,266 --> 00:08:39,066
and it is not about building an entire system

289
00:08:39,166 --> 00:08:40,166
Just ten minutes

290
00:08:40,266 --> 00:08:41,166
In ten minutes

291
00:08:41,166 --> 00:08:43,233
can you open the document and write three sentences

292
00:08:43,333 --> 00:08:45,100
can you explain one idea from the course

293
00:08:45,100 --> 00:08:46,566
out loud to the air once

294
00:08:46,700 --> 00:08:49,133
can you drag the video clips onto the timeline

295
00:08:49,266 --> 00:08:50,300
can you put on your shoes

296
00:08:50,300 --> 00:08:51,233
and walk downstairs

297
00:08:51,400 --> 00:08:53,466
can you send that question you have been afraid to ask

298
00:08:53,533 --> 00:08:54,833
to someone who knows

299
00:08:55,066 --> 00:08:56,666
The smaller this action is the better

300
00:08:56,766 --> 00:08:58,966
Small enough that you would feel embarrassed to say you have no time

301
00:08:59,100 --> 00:09:01,233
Small enough that it will not trigger too much fear

302
00:09:01,333 --> 00:09:03,466
Small enough that you do not need to become another person first

303
00:09:03,566 --> 00:09:04,733
The you who exists right now

304
00:09:04,733 --> 00:09:05,766
can already begin

305
00:09:05,900 --> 00:09:07,200
Many plans fail

306
00:09:07,266 --> 00:09:08,733
not because the goal is too big

307
00:09:08,733 --> 00:09:10,033
but because the entrance is too heavy

308
00:09:10,200 --> 00:09:12,233
You make the beginning too elaborate

309
00:09:12,333 --> 00:09:13,733
You need a full block of time

310
00:09:13,733 --> 00:09:15,033
You need to be in a good state

311
00:09:15,100 --> 00:09:16,366
You need a clean desk

312
00:09:16,366 --> 00:09:17,733
You need all the materials ready

313
00:09:17,766 --> 00:09:19,066
You need no one to interrupt you

314
00:09:19,166 --> 00:09:20,933
As a result the moments when you can truly start

315
00:09:20,933 --> 00:09:21,833
are painfully few

316
00:09:22,066 --> 00:09:23,300
A truly useful plan

317
00:09:23,300 --> 00:09:24,666
does not fill the whole day

318
00:09:24,700 --> 00:09:26,200
it makes starting lighter

319
00:09:26,400 --> 00:09:27,800
You can keep your plans

320
00:09:27,900 --> 00:09:29,366
but after every planned task

321
00:09:29,366 --> 00:09:31,666
attach one small action you can do immediately

322
00:09:31,800 --> 00:09:32,600
Reading

323
00:09:32,600 --> 00:09:35,066
becomes open to page twelve and read for five minutes

324
00:09:35,233 --> 00:09:35,966
Writing

325
00:09:35,966 --> 00:09:38,166
becomes write one ugly opening first

326
00:09:38,266 --> 00:09:39,066
Exercise

327
00:09:39,066 --> 00:09:41,400
becomes put on your shoes and walk to the corner

328
00:09:41,566 --> 00:09:42,400
Learning English

329
00:09:42,400 --> 00:09:44,800
becomes read the sentence from yesterday ten times

330
00:09:44,866 --> 00:09:46,366
Once the action becomes concrete

331
00:09:46,400 --> 00:09:48,600
procrastination loses one more place to hide

332
00:09:48,666 --> 00:09:51,266
If a planned task cannot be turned into a ten minute action

333
00:09:51,333 --> 00:09:52,966
do not put it into tomorrow yet

334
00:09:53,100 --> 00:09:54,433
because that is not a plan

335
00:09:54,500 --> 00:09:56,666
it is only a wish with no entrance yet

336
00:09:56,766 --> 00:09:58,066
You will slowly notice

337
00:09:58,200 --> 00:09:59,833
a plan is not better just because it is fuller

338
00:09:59,966 --> 00:10:02,366
Life is not more hopeful because it is packed tighter

339
00:10:02,466 --> 00:10:03,333
Often

340
00:10:03,500 --> 00:10:05,100
what really pushes you forward

341
00:10:05,100 --> 00:10:06,800
is not that beautiful schedule

342
00:10:06,800 --> 00:10:08,633
but an ordinary afternoon

343
00:10:08,700 --> 00:10:10,166
when you are not in the mood

344
00:10:10,200 --> 00:10:11,666
yet still write the first sentence

345
00:10:11,866 --> 00:10:13,300
That moment is not loud

346
00:10:13,300 --> 00:10:14,200
and it is not inspirational

347
00:10:14,266 --> 00:10:15,333
It may even feel a little clumsy

348
00:10:15,466 --> 00:10:17,400
But compared with a whole page of perfect plans

349
00:10:17,466 --> 00:10:19,133
it is closer to real change

350
00:10:19,200 --> 00:10:21,366
Next time you stare at your to do list

351
00:10:21,466 --> 00:10:23,333
do not rush to schedule it again

352
00:10:23,400 --> 00:10:24,633
Close the list

353
00:10:24,733 --> 00:10:26,166
Choose the smallest task

354
00:10:26,200 --> 00:10:27,333
Give it ten minutes

355
00:10:27,366 --> 00:10:28,433
After ten minutes

356
00:10:28,500 --> 00:10:30,133
decide whether you want to continue

357
00:10:30,266 --> 00:10:31,266
A lot of action

358
00:10:31,300 --> 00:10:33,266
does not begin once you have thought everything through

359
00:10:33,300 --> 00:10:34,400
It is after you begin

360
00:10:34,400 --> 00:10:35,833
that you slowly figure things out
